      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The given ellipse has a major axis of length 10, which divides it into halves, therefore we have $\\frac{10}{2}=5$. So, $a=5$. Similarly, $b=4$. The value of the semi-major axis is half of the major axis length, or $\\frac{a}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}$. The value of the semi-minor axis is half of minor axis length, or $\\frac{b}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2$. Plotting a right angle triangle and using the Pythagorean Theorem results in $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(a^2-b^2)\\right)=h$, where $h$ represents the horizontal distance between focus and vertex. Plugging the values of $a$ and $b$, we have $\\frac{1}{2}(5^2-4^2)=\\frac{1}{2}(25-16)=\\frac{9}{2}$.\nPlotting a line with slope of $\\frac{1}{2}$ through focus $(5,0)$, going off all the way to the right, to get point $C$ (origin). The vertical distance $|h|=3$, giving us $\\left(\\frac{3}{1}+(5/2)\\right)^2=13.25$. This represents the circle's radius.\nThus, the radius of the circle is $\\boxed{\\frac{9}{2}}$",
