      "text": "The center of an ellipse is at the center of the rectangle that contains it.  If one axis of our ellipse is on the x-axis, the other one is on the y-axis.  Thus, our ellipse has a center at (5, 4).\n\nLet's call f the focus.  Because the circle is tangent to the ellipse, the distance from (5, 4) to f (the center of the circle) is rad - r.\n\nConstruct radii of the ellipse,  best approximated by lines straight to (4, 8) and (5, 3).\n[asy]\nunitsize (6 cm);\n\npair F, E;\n\nF = (3, 3);\nE = (5, 4);\n\ndraw(E--F);\ndraw(arc(E, 4, 0, 180), red);\n\ndot(\"$\\\\text{Major}$\", F, S);\ndot(\"$\\\\text{Minor}$\", E, N);\ndot(\"$\\\\text{Foci Answer}$\", (1.1, 2.5), NW, red);\n[/asy]\nThe first radius (from (4, 8) to f) is 8 by distance formula.\nThe second radius (from (5, 3) to f) is from slope of perpendicular lines between (4, 8) and (5, 3) [and a distance formula.\n\nThe radius of the circle is one more than this radius\none radius = 6\nr + 1 = 6\nr = 5\nOur radius is 6 - 1, or 5.\n\nA related problem is the triangular region under the x-axis between the circle and elllaipse (both centres at origin and a radius of $ \\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{17}$ respectively.)\n\nA good start is always get and understand a picture.  Here, we see that the question is asking for the distance between the foci of the major and minor axes of the ellipse.  Therefore, it's easy to see that the answer is 2.",
